          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            While
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            considers deterministic evolution of particles and fields, quantum physics follows nondeterministic evolution where the probability of various outcomes of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurement"} measurement
            may be predicted from the state in a Hilbert space representing the possible reality: that state undergoes a unitary evolution, what means that the generator of the evolution is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msqrt
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sqrt{-1}
            times a Hermitean operator called the
            %em quantum Hamiltonian
            or the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+operator"} Hamiltonian operator
            of the system. The theoretical framework for describing this precisely is the
            = succeed "." do
              %strong quantum mechanics
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo →
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h\to 0
            lead to classical mechanical systems (not all: some phenomena including non-integer spin are purely quantum mechanical, but the properties depending on their existence survive in the “classical” limit); in limited generality, one can motivate and find the
            %em nonfunctorial
            procedure to single out a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+inverse"} right inverse
            to taking this
            %em classical limit
            under the name
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
          %p
            While quantum mechanics may be formulated for a wide range of physical systems, interpreted as particles, extended particles and fields, the quantum mechanics of fields is often called the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            and the quantum mechanics of systems of a fixed finite number of particles is often viewed as the quantum mechanics in a narrow sense.
          %h5#npov nPOV
          %p
            Mathematically, despite the basic formalism of quantum mechanics which is sound and clear, there are two big areas which are yet not clear. One is to understand quantization, in all cases – of particles, fields, strings and so on. The second and possibly more central to nLab is a problem how to define rigorously a wide range of quantum field theories and some related quantum mechanical systems like the hypothetical
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring+theory"} superstring theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interpretation+of+quantum+mechanics"} interpretation of quantum mechanics
            via the picture which is a special case of a FQFT, and where the time evolution functorially leads to evolution operators.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p We discuss some basic notions of quantum mechanics.
          %h3#quantum_mechanical_systems Quantum mechanical systems
          %p
            Recall the notion of a
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanical+system"} classical mechanical system
            %em commutative
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+algebra"} Poisson algebra
          #QuantumSystem.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong quantum mechanical system
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+algebra"} star algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A, (-)^\ast)
              over the
              = succeed "s." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+number"} complex number
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              of of quantum mechanical systems is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
              of
              = succeed "-algebras:" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi QuantMechSys
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mo *
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Alg
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mi op
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    QuantMechSys := {\ast}Alg_{\mathbb{C}}^{op}
                    \\,.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              It makes sense to think of this as a deformed version of a real
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+algebra"} Poisson algebra
              as follows:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  the Poisson-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie bracket
                  of a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+algebra"} Poisson algebra
                  corresponds to the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutator"} commutator
                  of the
                  = succeed "-algebra:" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo *
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi a
                        %mo ,
                        %mi b
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo :
                        %mo =
                        %mi a
                        %mi b
                        %mo −
                        %mi b
                        %mi a
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo ,
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        [a,b] := a b - b a
                        \\,,
              %li
                %p
                  the commutative algebra structure of the Poisson algebra coresponds to the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan+algebra"} Jordan algebra
                  structure of the
                  = succeed "-algebra," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo *
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mo ,
                        %mi b
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo :
                        %mo =
                        %mi a
                        %mi b
                        %mo +
                        %mi b
                        %mi a
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo .
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        (a,b) := a b + b a
                        \\,.
            %p
              With this interpretation the
              = succeed "-property" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivation"} derivation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,b,c \in A
              we have
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    [a,(b,c)] = ([a,b],c) + (b,[a,c])
                    \\,.
            %p
              We thus may regard a non-commutative star-algebra as a
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonassociative+algebra"} non-associative
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+algebra"} Poisson algebra
              \: a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan-Lie+algebra"} Jordan-Lie algebra
          %h3#observables_and_states Observables and states
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              Given a quantum mechanical system in terms of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+algebra"} star algebra
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  an
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable"} observable
                  is an element
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in A
                  such that
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi a
                            %mo *
                          %mo =
                          %mi a
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a^\ast = a
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state"} state
                  is a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+function"} linear function
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ρ
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                        %mo →
                        %mi ℂ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho : A \to \mathbb{C}
                  which is
                  %em positive
                  in the sense that for all
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in A
                  we have
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi ρ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi a
                          %msup
                            %mi a
                            %mo *
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ≥
                          %mn 0
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mo ↪
                          %mi ℂ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho(a a^\ast) \geq 0 \in \mathbb{R} \hookrightarrow \mathbb{C}
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              One can formalize the idea that a quantum mechanical system is like a deformed
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanical+system"} classical mechanical system
              as follows:
            %p
              To every
              = succeed "-algebra" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mrow
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {}^\ast
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is associated its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset+of+commutative+subalgebras"} poset of commutative subalgebras
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Com
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Com(A)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanical+system"} classical mechanical system
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
              to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+topos"} sheaf topos
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Sh
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Com
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(Com(A))
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  The
                  = succeed "-algebra" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo *
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %munder
                            %mi A
                            %mo ̲
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi Sh
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi Com
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi A
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline A \in Sh(Com(A))
              %li
                %p
                  the (classical)
                  = succeed "s" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state"} state
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %munder
                          %mi A
                          %mo ̲
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{A}
                  in
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Sh
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Com
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(Com(A))
                  are in natural bijection with the quantum states externally on
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              %li
                %p
                  the (classical)
                  = succeed "s" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable"} observable
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %munder
                          %mi A
                          %mo ̲
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{A}
                  in
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Sh
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Com
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(Com(A))
                  correspond to the external quantum observables on
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %p (…details…)
          %p
            One also says that the internal classical mechanical system
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Com
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %munder
                    %mi A
                    %mo ̲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Sh(Com(A)), \underline{A})
            is the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohrification"}> Bohrification
            ” of the external quantum system
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %h3#spaces_of_states Spaces of states
          %p
            Given a
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            together with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state"} state
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            on it, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GNS+construction"} GNS construction
            provides an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+product+space"} inner product space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mi ρ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_\rho
            together with an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mi ρ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_\rho
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector"} vector
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo =
                  %msqrt
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega = \sqrt(\rho)
            – the
            %span.newWikiWord
              vacuum vector
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/vacuum+vector"} ?
            – such that for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in A
            the value of the state
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho : A \to \mathbb{C}
            is obtained by applying
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msqrt
                    %mi ρ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sqrt{\rho}
            and then taking the inner product with
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msqrt
                      %mi ρ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sqrt \rho
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msqrt
                    %mi ρ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi a
                  %msqrt
                    %mi ρ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\rho(A) = \langle \sqrt\rho, a \sqrt \rho\rangle
                  \\,.
          %p
            If the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+algebra"} star algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            happens to be a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebra"} C-star algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
          %p Historically and still often in the literature, such a Hilbert space is taken as a fundamental input of the definition of quantum systems.
          %p
            Traditionally,
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac"} Dirac
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\psi\rangle
            represents a state and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle\psi|
            represents its linear adjoint. State evolutions are expressed as unitary maps. Self-adjoint operators represent physical quantities such as position and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/momentum"} momentum
            and are called
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurements"} Measurements
            are expressed as sets of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projectors"} projectors
            onto the eigenvectors of an observable.
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mixed+state"} mixed state
            quantum mechanics, physical states are represented as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/density+operators"} density operators
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo ↦
                  %msup
                    %mi U
                    %mo †
                  %mi ρ
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho \mapsto U^\dagger \rho U
            for unitary maps
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurements"} measurements
            are positive operator-valued measures (POVM’s). There is a natural embedding of pure states into the space of density matrices:
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo ↦
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\psi\rangle \mapsto |\psi\rangle\langle\psi|
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho = \sum_i a_i |\psi_i\rangle\langle\psi_i|
          %p
            Composite systems are formed by taking the tensor product of Hilbert spaces. If a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+state"} pure state
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi Ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\Psi\rangle \in H_1 \otimes H_2
            can be written as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\psi_1\rangle \otimes |\psi_2\rangle
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\psi_i\rangle \in H_i
            it is said to be
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separable"} separable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\psi_i\rangle
            exist,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi Ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\Psi\rangle
            is said to be
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/entangled"} entangled
          %h3#flows_and_time_evolution Flows and time evolution
          %p
            As for classical mechanics, 1-parameter families of flows in a quantum mechanical system are induced from observables
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in A
            by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mi d
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mi λ
                  %msub
                    %mi b
                    %mi λ
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi ℏ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi b
                    %mi λ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{d}{d \lambda} b_\lambda = \frac{1}{i \hbar}[b_\lambda, a]
                  \\,.
          %p
            In a non-relativistic system one specifies an observable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            – called the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
            – whose flow represents the time evolution of the system. (This is the
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+picture"} Heisenberg picture
          %p
            We comment on how to interpret this from the point of view of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
          %p
            Quantum mechanics of point particles may be understood as a special case of the formalism of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            of point particles. Of course, we can take a configuration space of a system of particles looking like the configuration space of a single particle in a higher dimensional manifold.
          %p
            Remark: related query on the relation between QFT and quantum mechanics (of particles and in general) can be found
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.ntnu.no/~stacey/Mathforge/nForum/comments.php?DiscussionID=2573&Focus=22060#Comment_22060"} here
          %p
            One may usefully think of the quantum mechanics of a point particle propagating on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            as being
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0+1)
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
          %p
            the fields of this system are maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma \to X
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Riem
                  %msub
                    %mi Bord
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma \in Riem Bord_1
            are 1-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism"} cobordism
            %em trajectories
            of the particle.
          %p
            After
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            this yields a 1-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            given by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi Riem
                  %msub
                    %mi Bord
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi Hilb
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  U(-) : Riem Bord_1 \to Hilb
          %p
            from
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism"} cobordism
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            = succeed "s)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                to the point the
                %em space of states
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℋ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
                = succeed "-sections" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi L
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_2
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+metric"} Riemannian metric
                on
                = succeed ")" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                under which the particle in question is charged
            %li
              %p
                to the cobordism of Riemannian length
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
                the operator
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mo =
                      %mi exp
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mfrac
                          %mi t
                          %mrow
                            %mi i
                            %mi ℏ
                        %mi H
                        %mo )
                      %mo :
                      %mi ℋ
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℋ
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      U(t) := \exp\left(\frac{t}{i \hbar } H \right)
                      \: \mathcal{H} \to \mathcal{H}
                      \\,,
              %p
                where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
                is the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+operator"} Hamiltonian operator
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mo ∇
                        %mo †
                      %mo ∘
                      %mo ∇
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H = \nabla^\dagger \circ \nabla
                for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∇
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+derivative"} covariant derivative
                of the given background
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
          %p
            Such a setup describes the quantum mechanics of a particle that feels forces of backgound
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            encoded in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+metric"} Riemannian metric
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and forces of background gauge fields (such as the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∇
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
          %p
            (This is the
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schr%C3%B6dinger+picture"} Schrödinger picture
          %h3#Subsystems Quantum subsystems
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
              an algebra describing a quantum system, def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#QuantumSystem"}
              %strong subsystem
              is a subalgebra (a
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject"} subobject
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi 𝒜
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \hookrightarrow \mathcal{A}
            %p
              Two subsystems
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_1, B_2 \hookrightarrow \mathcal{A}
              are called
              %strong independent subsystems
              if the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+map"} linear map
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 2
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    B_1 \otimes B_2 \to \mathcal{A}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↦
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⋅
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (b_1, b_2) \mapsto b_1 \cdot b_2
            %p
              from the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
              of algebras (the
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composite+system"} composite system
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 2
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∨
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    B_1 \otimes B_2 \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} B_1 \vee B_2 \hookrightarrow \mathcal{A}
            %p
              through the algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∨
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_1 \vee B_2
              that is generated by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_1
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_2
              inside
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
              (the smallest subalgebra containing both).
          %p
            See for instance (
            %a{:href => "#BrunettiFredenhagen"}> BrunettiFredenhagen, section 5.2.2
            ).
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_5 Definition
            %p
              Given two independent subsystems
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi B
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi B
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi 𝒜
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_1, B_2 \hookrightarrow \mathcal{A}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/states"} states
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ρ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho_1 : B_1 \to \mathbb{C}
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ρ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi B
                        %mn 2
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℂ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho_2 : B_2 \to \mathbb{C}
              %strong product state
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ρ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mi ρ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho_1 \otimes \rho_2
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∨
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_1 \vee B_2
              is defined to be
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi ρ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mi ρ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↦
                    %msub
                      %mi ρ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mi ρ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\rho_1 \otimes \rho_2) :
                    (b_1 , b_2) \mapsto \rho_1(b_1) \rho_2(b_2)
                    \\,.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_6 Definition
            %p
              There exist states on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∨
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_1 \vee B_2
              that are not (convex combinations of) product states. This phenomenon is called
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/entanglement"} entanglement
          %h2#formulations_and_formalization Formulations and formalization
          %h3#ordertheoretic_structure_in_quantum_mechanics Order-theoretic structure in quantum mechanics
          %p
            See
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/order-theoretic+structure+in+quantum+mechanics"} order-theoretic structure in quantum mechanics
          %h3#quantum_mechanics_in_terms_of_compact_categories
            Quantum mechanics in terms of
            = succeed "-compact" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo †
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dagger
          %p
            Many aspects of quantum mechanics and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computation"} quantum computation
            depend only on the abstract properties of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilb"} Hilb
            characterized by the fact that it is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%80%A0-compact+category"} †-compact category
          %p For more on this see
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics+in+terms+of+%E2%80%A0-compact+categories"}> quantum mechanics in terms of †-compact categories
              \.
          %h2#foundational_theorems_of_quantum_mechanics Foundational theorems of quantum mechanics
          %p
            The following circle of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theorems"} theorems
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kochen-Specker+theorem"} Kochen-Specker theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gleason%27s+theorem"} Gleason's theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alfsen-Shultz+theorem"} Alfsen-Shultz theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Harding-D%C3%B6ring-Hamhalter+theorem"} Harding-Döring-Hamhalter theorem
          %p
            all revolve around the phenomenon that the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"}> phase space
            ” in quantum mechanics and hence the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} space of quantum states
            are all determined by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan+algebra"} Jordan algebra
            structure on the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+observables"} algebra of observables
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset+of+commutative+subalgebras"} poset of commutative subalgebras
            of the algebra of observables. See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/order-theoretic+structure+in+quantum+mechanics"} order-theoretic structure in quantum mechanics
            for more on this.
          %p There is also
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wigner%27s+theorem"} Wigner's theorem
          %p
            which says roughly that linear maps between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces+of+quantum+states"} spaces of quantum states
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+operators"} unitary operators
            (or anti-unitary) already when they preserve
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/norm"} norm
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability"} probability
          %h2#applications_of_quantum_mechanics Applications of quantum mechanics
          %p
            Quantum mechanics, as opposed to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                In
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chemistry"} chemistry
                (“
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+chemistry"}> quantum chemistry
                ”) the properties of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atoms"} atoms
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/molecules"} molecules
                are derived from quantum mechanics.
            %li
              %p
                In
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solid+state+physics"} solid state physics
                the properties of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metals"} metals
                etc. are described by quantum mechanics of electron gases.
            %li
              %p
                In
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle+physics"} particle physics
                of course,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
                is the appropriate description.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/harmonic+oscillator"} harmonic oscillator
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hydrogen+atom"} hydrogen atom
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+lattice+system"} quantum lattice system
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiclassical+approximation"} semiclassical approximation
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"}> deformation quantization
                  ,
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            %li
              %p
                %strong quantum mechanics
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schr%C3%B6dinger+picture"}> Schrödinger picture
                    ,
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+picture"} Heisenberg picture
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+picture"} Dirac picture
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observable"}> quantum observable
                    ,
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uncertainty+principle"} uncertainty principle
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state"} quantum state
                    (
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+quantum+state"}> pure
                    ,
                    = succeed ")" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mixed+quantum+state"} mixed
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+probability"} quantum probability
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propagator"} propagator
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+superposition"}> quantum superposition
                    ,
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+fluctuation"} quantum fluctuation
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+entanglement"} quantum entanglement
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive-operator+valued+probability+measure"} positive-operator valued probability measure
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometrical+formulation+of+quantum+mechanics"} geometrical formulation of quantum mechanics
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hidden+variable+theory"}> hidden variable theory
                    ,
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/EPR+paradox"}> EPR paradox
                        ,
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bell%27s+inequalities"} Bell's inequalities
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kochen-Specker+theorem"} Kochen-Specker theorem
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/no-cloning+theorem"} no-cloning theorem
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Born-Oppenheimer+approximation"} Born-Oppenheimer approximation
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+tunneling"} quantum tunneling
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetric+quantum+mechanics"} supersymmetric quantum mechanics
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+logic"}> quantum logic
                    ,
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computing"} quantum computing
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"}> linear logic
                      ,
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+type+theory"} linear type theory
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+quantum+mechanics+in+terms+of+dagger-compact+categories"} finite quantum mechanics in terms of dagger-compact categories
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/JBW-algebraic+quantum+mechanics"} JBW-algebraic quantum mechanics
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interpretation+of+quantum+mechanics"} interpretation of quantum mechanics
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quaternionic+quantum+mechanics"} quaternionic quantum mechanics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"}> FQFT
                  ,
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+chemistry"} quantum chemistry
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+biology"} quantum biology
          %h2#References References
          %h3#historical_origins Historical origins
          %p Historical review:
          %ul
            %li
              Eren Volkan Küçük:
              %em The Birth of Quantum Mechanics: A Historical Study Through the Canonical Papers
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.13630"}> arXiv:2503.13630
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p The seed of quantum mechanics was sown in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Max+Planck"} Max Planck
              (transl. M. Martius)
              %em The Theory of Heat Radiation
              (1914) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://www.gutenberg.org/files/40030/40030-pdf.pdf"}> pdf
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            with the recognition of a quantum of “action”:
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
            (
            %a{:href => "https://www.gutenberg.org/files/40030/40030-pdf.pdf#page=178"}> p. 164
            )
          %p Quantum mechanics as such originates with:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Werner+Heisenberg"}> Werner Heisenberg
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Über quantentheoretische Umdeutung kinematischer und mechanischer Beziehungen
                %strong 33
                (1925) 879–893 &amp;lbrack;
